dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: a non-blocking connect the program gives up on

Applies on top of the other dante-*.patch files.

For a non-blocking connect(2) libdsocks gives the program a placeholder
socket and keeps the real one (connected to the proxy, being negotiated by
the connect-child) as the entry's control descriptor; sigio() dup2()s it
onto the program's descriptor when the negotiation finishes. libdsocks does
not interpose close(2), and programs routinely close sockets whose connect
takes too long. Then:

- sigio() dup2()ed the proxy socket onto the closed descriptor's number
  anyway, i.e. over whatever the program had opened under that number
  since: with transmission, the files it downloads into ("Invalid seek" on
  pwrite(), data received but never written, torrents not progressing);
- nothing ever closed the control descriptor: the daemon accumulated tens of
  thousands of sockets (CLOSE-WAIT to the proxy, and closed ones).

The entry now records the identity (st_dev/st_ino) of the placeholder.
sigio() only completes the connect if the program's descriptor is still
that socket; otherwise it drops the entry. socks_rmaddr() closes the control
descriptor of a connect still in progress, a new connect on a reused
number frees a stale entry's control descriptor, and socks_addrisours() no
longer takes a reused descriptor for the old connect in progress.

Test: test/fdreusecheck.c (via test/run.sh).

